  "raw": {
    "https://emmanuel.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205656": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Nursing",
      "description": "Emmanuel College\nJob Posting: Assistant/Associate Professor of Nursing Emmanuel College \n\tJob Posting: Assistant/Associate Professor of Nursing Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant/Associate Professor of Nursing Category: Academics Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Nursing Locations: Boston, MA Posted: Oct 9, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Position ID: 205656 Share About Emmanuel College: Emmanuel College-a Catholic, coeducational, residential, liberal arts and sciences college in Boston-has been educating critical thinkers, ethical decision makers and community leaders since 1919. Situated in Boston's Fenway neighborhood within the Longwood Medical and Academic Area, Emmanuel's 17-acre campus is home to nearly 2,000 undergraduate and graduate students. The College provides boundless opportunities for students to expand their worldview through rigorous coursework, significant internship and career opportunities throughout the Boston area and beyond, collaborations with distinguished and dedicated faculty, and participation in a dynamic campus community. Emmanuel's more than 70 programs in the arts, humanities, education, natural sciences, business and nursing foster spirited discourse and substantive learning experiences that honor the College's commitment to educate the whole person and prepare students to emerge as tomorrow's leaders. For more information, visit www.emmanuel.edu. Job Description: The Emmanuel College of Nursing invites applications for an Assistant/Associate Professor of Nursing to begin January 2026. Rank will be determined based on prior experience. This is a non-tenure-track, 9-month faculty position with teaching responsibilities in the in-person BSN program. The School of Nursing currently offers programs leading to BSN, RN to BSN completion, and an MSN in nursing education. The School of Nursing programs are accredited by the Commission on Collegiate Nursing Education. Requirements: Applicants must have a Massachusetts nursing license in good standing and hold a BSN and MS in Nursing. A doctorate, PhD or DNP, is strongly preferred. The applicant must possess a minimum of two years full-time nursing experience, or its equivalent, within the last five years and demonstrate evidence of clinical expertise appropriate to the teaching responsibilities. Applicants should have experience participating in scholarly activities. Teaching experience in higher education required. Applicants must demonstrate strong teaching skills at the undergraduate level. Faculty in this program are expected to teach, advise, mentor, evaluate students, conduct clinical, laboratory, face-to-face, hybrid, and/or on-line student learning activities designed to meet student and program learning outcomes. Experience in curricula development and CCNE accreditation preferred. Faculty shall participate in the governance of Emmanuel College and the School of Nursing, as appropriate, for the accomplishment of the goals of the College and School of Nursing. Faculty are expected to develop, implement, and evaluate policies that lead to improvement in student outcomes. In addition, they are responsible for contributing to ongoing development and evaluation of curricula. Additional Information: Emmanuel College values a welcoming and supportive community for all. We encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply and are committed to fair and equitable hiring practices based on qualifications and experience. Unless otherwise stated above, this is an on-campus position. This is not a remote or hybrid position as employees are essential in order to provide a fully on-campus, residential college experience for our students and the community. The posted salary range reflects the good-faith compensation estimate for this position. The actual pay offered will be based on the candidate's skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity factors.  $98,000-123,000. Application Instructions: A complete application must include a cover letter, resume, statement of teaching philosophy,  a statement of purpose, and contact information for three references. In your statement of purpose, please describe how your values, experiences, and professional goals align with Emmanuel College's mission to educate and inspire students in the spirit of the Sisters of Notre Dame de Namur, who are committed to social justice, human dignity, and transformative education for all. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Emmanuel College 400 The Fenway, Boston, MA 02115"
    },
    "https://emmanuel.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205489": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty - Education",
      "description": "Emmanuel College\nJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty - Education Emmanuel College \n\tJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty - Education Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Adjunct Faculty - Education Category: Academics Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Education Locations: Boston, MA Posted: Oct 5, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Part-time Position ID: 205489 Share About Emmanuel College: Emmanuel College-a Catholic, coeducational, residential, liberal arts and sciences college in Boston-has been educating critical thinkers, ethical decision makers and community leaders since 1919. Situated in Boston's Fenway neighborhood within the Longwood Medical and Academic Area, Emmanuel's 17-acre campus is home to nearly 2,000 undergraduate and graduate students. The College provides boundless opportunities for students to expand their worldview through rigorous coursework, significant internship and career opportunities throughout the Boston area and beyond, collaborations with distinguished and dedicated faculty, and participation in a dynamic campus community. Emmanuel's more than 70 programs in the arts, humanities, education, natural sciences, business and nursing foster spirited discourse and substantive learning experiences that honor the College's commitment to educate the whole person and prepare students to emerge as tomorrow's leaders. For more information, visit www.emmanuel.edu. Job Description: Emmanuel College invites applications to the Department of Education for an adjunct to teach two related undergraduate courses in SP27: Teaching students with disabilities for general education practitioners (meets weekly Tuesday night 4:30-7:30) and Moderate Disabilities Internship and Seminar (meets every other Wednesday night from 4:15-7:15). The combination of the two courses supports students in seeking an additional license in Moderate disabilities PreK-8, 5-12 to their initial license. Students can also take the Tuesday night course separately as an Education elective. Responsibilities include: Preparing for and teaching in person classes (see time frames above) Holding student hours Preparing and grading assignments, lesson plans, and projects Teaching Students with disabilities for general education practitioners Course objectives: 1. To recognize each of thirteen disability categories identified by IDEA-2004; demonstrate knowledge of the characteristics of each disability, issues of definition, causation and prevalence, and validated school- based practices. (Competencies: 1, 3, 5, 6) 2. To identify the essential provisions of IDEA-04, MA Special Ed. Regulations, the Rehabilitation Act (Section 504), the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA), and Every Student Succeeds Act (ESSA) and their implied responsibilities for general educators.  (Competencies: 1, 3, 5, 7) 3. To demonstrate an understanding of racial, cultural and linguistic diversity in the education of students with special needs. (Competencies: 1, 5, 6) 4. To describe the use of technology to support the inclusion of students with disabilities in school.  (Competencies: 3, 4) 5. To gain an understanding of curriculum modifications, accommodations, and adaptations for students of diverse learning styles. (Competencies: 1, 3, 5, 6) 6. To understand the benefits of collaborative teaching, co-teaching and effective personnel management within the classroom for all students.  (Competencies: 1, 3, 5, 6) 7. To complete fieldwork in a moderate disabilities classroom.  (Competencies: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7) 8. To maintain a reflective journal of their field assignment. (Competencies: 1, 3, 5, 6) Moderate Disabilities Internship and Seminar: Students participate in 150 hour practicum experience in an inclusion, resource, or self-contained classroom under the direction of a licensed special educator and a college program supervisor as part of this course. Course objectives: To provide an open and safe forum for students to share and discuss experiences relating to their work supporting students receiving special education services. To provide a forum for discussion of observed teaching strategies that support academic, social, and behavioral needs of students. To discuss applications of assessment and differentiation that provide students opportunities for growth in academics, social skills and behavior management. To provide opportunities to discuss the application of state and federal regulations as seen in school experiences. To provide time and support to candidates as they select evidence pieces that meet licensing criteria. Requirements: Licensure in Moderate disabilities, expertise in teaching students with students with moderate disabilities and working with PreK-Grade 12 learners, deep understanding and experience with the special education competencies as outlined by MA DESE, experience working with undergraduate students and/or preservice or inservice teachers. Additional Information: Emmanuel College values a welcoming and supportive community for all. We encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply and are committed to fair and equitable hiring practices based on qualifications and experience. Unless otherwise stated above, this is an on-campus position. This is not a remote or hybrid position as employees are essential in order to provide a fully on-campus, residential college experience for our students and the community. The posted salary range reflects the good-faith compensation estimate for this position. The actual pay offered will be based on the candidate's skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity factors.  $4,950-$5,500 per course Application Instructions: A complete application must include a cover letter, resume, a statement of purpose, and contact information for three references. In your statement of purpose, please describe how your values, experiences, and professional goals align with Emmanuel College's mission to educate and inspire students in the spirit of the Sisters of Notre Dame de Namur, who are committed to social justice, human dignity, and transformative education for all. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Emmanuel College 400 The Fenway, Boston, MA 02115"
    },
