    "https://emmanuel.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=206231": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty - Art Education",
      "description": "Emmanuel College\nJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty -  Art Education Emmanuel College \n\tJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty -  Art Education Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Adjunct Faculty -  Art Education Category: Academics Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Art Locations: Boston, MA Posted: Oct 28, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Part-time Position ID: 206231 Share About Emmanuel College: Emmanuel College-a Catholic, coeducational, residential, liberal arts and sciences college in Boston-has been educating critical thinkers, ethical decision makers and community leaders since 1919. Situated in Boston's Fenway neighborhood within the Longwood Medical and Academic Area, Emmanuel's 17-acre campus is home to nearly 2,000 undergraduate and graduate students. The College provides boundless opportunities for students to expand their worldview through rigorous coursework, significant internship and career opportunities throughout the Boston area and beyond, collaborations with distinguished and dedicated faculty, and participation in a dynamic campus community. Emmanuel's more than 70 programs in the arts, humanities, education, natural sciences, business and nursing foster spirited discourse and substantive learning experiences that honor the College's commitment to educate the whole person and prepare students to emerge as tomorrow's leaders. For more information, visit www.emmanuel.edu. Job Description: Emmanuel College invites applications to the Art department in collaboration with the Education Department for an adjunct position to teach an undergraduate course SP27: Methods and Materials for Teaching Art (Grades 5-12) on M/W 4:15-5:30. This course is required for Secondary Education/Studio Art double majors pursuing MA licensure to teach visual arts Grades 5-12. Responsibilities include: Preparing for and teaching in person classes (see time frames above) Holding student hours (1 hour per class) Preparing and grading assignments, lesson plans, and projects Course Goals and Outcomes Goals: Develop an understanding of best practices in teaching art in 5-12. Understand stages of childhood development and the relationship to artistic development. Explore content, process, and context as they apply to art instruction, and to student learning in 5-12. Develop an understanding of effective approaches in the design, delivery, and assessment of art instruction. Become aware of a variety of successful teaching methods that lead to the realization of curriculum goals. Gain confidence through knowledge and skills for teaching art from 5-12 centers. Understand the value of the art process in numerous settings and populations. Develop and understand studio and time management As a result of participating in this course, students will be able to: Demonstrate knowledge of appropriate instructional methods to meet the needs of secondary school students including special needs students. Demonstrate knowledge of effective instructional methods, classroom safety practices and management techniques as they relate to the use of specific media in art production. Demonstrate understanding of methods used to design art instruction that promotes visual literacy, interdisciplinary and cultural connections, and incorporates art history, criticism, aesthetics, visual culture, and art production. Demonstrate the use of effective assessment tools. Demonstrate knowledge and use of computer productivity tools in delivery of art instruction, and classroom management and organization. Observe and evaluate the practice of art instruction by participating in a 30-hour art practicum assignment. Requirements: Licensure in Visual Arts Grades 5-12, expertise in teaching students with and working with 5-Grade 12 learners, deep understanding and experience with the Visual Arts standards as outlined by MA DESE, experience working with undergraduate students and/or pre-service or in-service teachers. Additional Information: Emmanuel College values a welcoming and supportive community for all. We encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply and are committed to fair and equitable hiring practices based on qualifications and experience. Unless otherwise stated above, this is an on-campus position. This is not a remote or hybrid position as employees are essential in order to provide a fully on-campus, residential college experience for our students and the community. The posted salary range reflects the good-faith compensation estimate for this position. The actual pay offered will be based on the candidate's skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity factors.  $4,950-$5,500 per course (If enrollment is below 10 students, salary will be prorated) Application Instructions: A complete application must include a cover letter, resume, a statement of purpose, and contact information for three references. In your statement of purpose, please describe how your values, experiences, and professional goals align with Emmanuel College's mission to educate and inspire students in the spirit of the Sisters of Notre Dame de Namur, who are committed to social justice, human dignity, and transformative education for all. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Emmanuel College 400 The Fenway, Boston, MA 02115"
    },
    "https://emmanuel.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=206071": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty - Political Science",
      "description": "Emmanuel College\nJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty - Political Science Emmanuel College \n\tJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty - Political Science Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Adjunct Faculty - Political Science Category: Academics Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Political Science Locations: Boston, MA Posted: Oct 22, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Part-time Position ID: 206071 Share About Emmanuel College: Emmanuel College-a Catholic, coeducational, residential, liberal arts and sciences college in Boston-has been educating critical thinkers, ethical decision makers and community leaders since 1919. Situated in Boston's Fenway neighborhood within the Longwood Medical and Academic Area, Emmanuel's 17-acre campus is home to nearly 2,000 undergraduate and graduate students. The College provides boundless opportunities for students to expand their worldview through rigorous coursework, significant internship and career opportunities throughout the Boston area and beyond, collaborations with distinguished and dedicated faculty, and participation in a dynamic campus community. Emmanuel's more than 70 programs in the arts, humanities, education, natural sciences, business and nursing foster spirited discourse and substantive learning experiences that honor the College's commitment to educate the whole person and prepare students to emerge as tomorrow's leaders. For more information, visit www.emmanuel.edu. Job Description: The Department of Political Science and International Studies at Emmanuel College is seeking an adjunct faculty to teach one section of Introduction to American Politics and Government for the Spring 2027 semester. This course offers an overview of the American political system. Included are examinations of the American presidency, Congress, political parties, interest groups, the courts and the mass media. Students analyze the way in which American society attempts to realize the goals of a constitutional democracy, as well as the successes and failures of the system. This course meets Tuesdays and Thursdays 10:50AM - 12:05PM Requirements: Master's Degree in Political Science or a related field is required.  Experience teaching at the undergraduate level is preferred. Additional Information: Emmanuel College values a welcoming and supportive community for all. We encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply and are committed to fair and equitable hiring practices based on qualifications and experience. Unless otherwise stated above, this is an on-campus position. This is not a remote or hybrid position as employees are essential in order to provide a fully on-campus, residential college experience for our students and the community. The posted salary range reflects the good-faith compensation estimate for this position. The actual pay offered will be based on the candidate's skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity factors.  $4,950-$5,500 per course Application Instructions: A complete application must include a cover letter, resume, a statement of purpose, and contact information for three references. In your statement of purpose, please describe how your values, experiences, and professional goals align with Emmanuel College's mission to educate and inspire students in the spirit of the Sisters of Notre Dame de Namur, who are committed to social justice, human dignity, and transformative education for all. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Emmanuel College 400 The Fenway, Boston, MA 02115"
    },
    "https://emmanuel.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205957": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty - Computer Science",
      "description": "Emmanuel College\nJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty - Computer Science Emmanuel College \n\tJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty - Computer Science Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Adjunct Faculty - Computer Science Category: Academics Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Business and Economics Locations: Boston, MA Posted: Oct 20, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Part-time Position ID: 205957 Share About Emmanuel College: Emmanuel College-a Catholic, coeducational, residential, liberal arts and sciences college in Boston-has been educating critical thinkers, ethical decision makers and community leaders since 1919. Situated in Boston's Fenway neighborhood within the Longwood Medical and Academic Area, Emmanuel's 17-acre campus is home to nearly 2,000 undergraduate and graduate students. The College provides boundless opportunities for students to expand their worldview through rigorous coursework, significant internship and career opportunities throughout the Boston area and beyond, collaborations with distinguished and dedicated faculty, and participation in a dynamic campus community. Emmanuel's more than 70 programs in the arts, humanities, education, natural sciences, business and nursing foster spirited discourse and substantive learning experiences that honor the College's commitment to educate the whole person and prepare students to emerge as tomorrow's leaders. For more information, visit www.emmanuel.edu. Job Description: The Business Department of Emmanuel College is seeking an adjunct faculty to teach one section of Practical Machine Learning and/or one section of Programming II and Introduction to Computer Science for the Spring 2027 semester. Both courses are customized to Emmanuel College's curriculum and fully developed. Both courses are a part of the Computer Science major and Data Analytics minor. Practical Machine Learning: The course provides an introduction to Artificial Intelligence (AI), Deep Learning, and Machine Learning (ML). A survey of multiple AI techniques, focusing heavily on ML. Students will learn about the different concepts behind each technique, experiment with interactive demonstrations, assess them for equity and bias, and apply them in their assignments. Techniques may be updated as the fast-moving field of machine learning evolves. This course does not go into extreme depth on ML theory, instead focusing on how to use these techniques to solve problems. The goal of this course is for students to understand what Machine Learning is and is not, and have a \"utility belt\" of skills and conceptual understanding to allow them to identify a problem, choose an AI technique, and apply it effectively. This course will be held on campus Mondays and Wednesdays 4:15 pm - 5:30 pm. Programming II and Introduction to Computer Science: This course builds on the experience of Introduction to Programming to introduce new ways of thinking via two paradigms: Functional and Object-Oriented programming. After both paradigms have been practiced in turn, the course introduces a new language, Python, to further exercise these ideas and provide a powerful set of tools for students to apply to their final projects. The Big Ideas include new programming concepts, language features, and tools, but also how these features combine in different systems, providing a generalizable foundation for moving forward in computer science. This course will be held on campus Tuesdays and Thursdays 4:30 pm - 5:45 pm. Responsibilities include: Teach the assigned course as designed. Assess student performance. Provide assistance and mentorship to students. **Please indicate in your cover letter for which course(s) you are interested in teaching** Requirements: Master's Degree in Computer Science or Mathematics or a closely related field, relevant professional experience, and a commitment to excellence in undergraduate teaching are required. Additional Information: Emmanuel College values a welcoming and supportive community for all. We encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply and are committed to fair and equitable hiring practices based on qualifications and experience. Unless otherwise stated above, this is an on-campus position. This is not a remote or hybrid position as employees are essential in order to provide a fully on-campus, residential college experience for our students and the community. The posted salary range reflects the good-faith compensation estimate for this position. The actual pay offered will be based on the candidate's skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity factors.  $4,950-$5,500 per course Application Instructions: A complete application must include a cover letter, resume, a statement of purpose, and contact information for three references. In your statement of purpose, please describe how your values, experiences, and professional goals align with Emmanuel College's mission to educate and inspire students in the spirit of the Sisters of Notre Dame de Namur, who are committed to social justice, human dignity, and transformative education for all. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Emmanuel College 400 The Fenway, Boston, MA 02115"
    },
    "https://emmanuel.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=206393": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty - Art",
      "description": "Emmanuel College\nJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty -  Art Emmanuel College \n\tJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty -  Art Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Adjunct Faculty -  Art Category: Academics Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Art Locations: Boston, MA Posted: Nov 4, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Part-time Position ID: 206393 Share About Emmanuel College: Emmanuel College-a Catholic, coeducational, residential, liberal arts and sciences college in Boston-has been educating critical thinkers, ethical decision makers and community leaders since 1919. Situated in Boston's Fenway neighborhood within the Longwood Medical and Academic Area, Emmanuel's 17-acre campus is home to nearly 2,000 undergraduate and graduate students. The College provides boundless opportunities for students to expand their worldview through rigorous coursework, significant internship and career opportunities throughout the Boston area and beyond, collaborations with distinguished and dedicated faculty, and participation in a dynamic campus community. Emmanuel's more than 70 programs in the arts, humanities, education, natural sciences, business and nursing foster spirited discourse and substantive learning experiences that honor the College's commitment to educate the whole person and prepare students to emerge as tomorrow's leaders. For more information, visit www.emmanuel.edu. Job Description: The Department of Art at Emmanuel College invites applications for an Adjunct Instructor to teach ART 2451: Ceramics I for the Spring 2027 semester. This course serves all art majors, art therapy majors, Ceramics minors, and is open to non-majors fulfilling their Visual Creative Inquiry general education credits. This course focuses on basic handbuilding skills and introductory knowledge. Term: January 13, 2027 - April 27, 2027 Schedule: Wednesday 1:25-4:05 (plus one weekly office hour) Requirements: Must hold an MFA in ceramics or a closely related studio field. A professional portfolio demonstrating a deep understanding of Ceramic art practices. Prior college-level teaching experience is preferred. Additional Information: Emmanuel College values a welcoming and supportive community for all. We encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply and are committed to fair and equitable hiring practices based on qualifications and experience. Unless otherwise stated above, this is an on-campus position. This is not a remote or hybrid position as employees are essential in order to provide a fully on-campus, residential college experience for our students and the community. The posted salary range reflects the good-faith compensation estimate for this position. The actual pay offered will be based on the candidate's skills, qualifications, experience, and internal equity factors.  $4,950-$5,500 per course Application Instructions: A complete application must include a cover letter, resume, a statement of purpose, and contact information for three references. In your statement of purpose, please describe how your values, experiences, and professional goals align with Emmanuel College's mission to educate and inspire students in the spirit of the Sisters of Notre Dame de Namur, who are committed to social justice, human dignity, and transformative education for all. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Emmanuel College 400 The Fenway, Boston, MA 02115"
    },
    "https://emmanuel.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=206392": {
